  "question": {
    "text": "The maximum distance from origin of a point on the curve $x=a \\sin t-b \\sin \\left(\\frac{a t}{b}\\right)$ \n$y=a \\cos t-b \\cos \\left(\\frac{a t}{b}\\right)$, both $a, b>0$ is",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "dHkqK",
      "text": "\na - b\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "Ez40S",
      "text": "\na + b\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "9JHX4",
      "text": "\n$\\sqrt{a^2+b^2}$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "u2SKZ",
      "text": "\n$\\sqrt{a^2-b^2}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "Distance of origin from $(x, y)=\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}$\n$$\n=\\sqrt{a^2+b^2-2 a b \\cos \\left(t-\\frac{a t}{b}\\right)}=\\sqrt{a^2+b^2-2 a b}\\left[\\because \\max \\cdot \\cos \\left(t-\\frac{a t}{b}\\right)=1\\right]=a-b\n$$",
    "image": null
  },
